{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 9,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.048,
            0.428,
            0.065
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.087,
            0.592,
            0.11
          ],
          "content": "Does it decrease by the same rate (or factor)?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.112,
            0.846,
            0.187
          ],
          "content": "Going by bicycle is 3 times faster than walking $( 1 5 \\div 5 )$ . The speed has increased 3 times. The travel time has decreased 3 times too $( 1 8 \\div 6 )$ The speed has increased by the same factor by which the travel time has decreased."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.189,
            0.701,
            0.212
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Check if this is the case for the other modes of transport."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.214,
            0.845,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": "Since both quantities, speed and time, change by the same factor, they are proportional. But they change in opposite directions, or inversely. Such proportions are called **inverse proportions**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.27,
            0.845,
            0.308
          ],
          "content": "From the table, we can see that the product of speed and time are the same for all modes of transport, namely 90 km."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.319,
            0.847,
            0.358
          ],
          "content": "relation of the type $x y = k ,$ where k is a constant."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.368,
            0.846,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": "In the previous example, if x represents the speed and y represents the time taken, then k is the distance between Lucknow and Kanpur, which remains constant."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.289,
            0.433,
            0.709,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.643,
            0.845,
            0.681
          ],
          "content": "Thus, if quantities x and y are inversely proportional, and $X _ { 1 }$ and $X _ { 2 }$ are values of x that have corresponding y values $y _ { 1 }$ and $y _ { 2 ^ { \\circ } }$ then"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.35,
            0.681,
            0.644,
            0.7
          ],
          "content": "x_{1}y_{1}=x_{2}y_{2}=k ( some constant ).",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.702,
            0.49,
            0.736
          ],
          "content": "From these we can see that $\\frac { \\overline { { X } } _ { 1 } } { X _ { _ 2 } } = \\frac { \\overline { { y } } _ { 2 } } { \\overline { { y } } _ { 1 } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.736,
            0.845,
            0.773
          ],
          "content": "Let us check if this is true for other modes of transport, such as walking and by car. Let us use x to represent speed and y to represent time."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.357,
            0.773,
            0.639,
            0.795
          ],
          "content": "x_{1}=5,x_{2}=60,y_{1}=18,y_{2}=1.5.",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.402,
            0.795,
            0.595,
            0.829
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array} { r } { \\frac { X _ { _ 1 } } { X _ { _ 2 } } = \\frac { 5 } { 6 0 } = 0 . 0 8 3 3 3 3 . . . } \\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.833,
            0.623,
            0.869
          ],
          "content": "What is the value of $\\frac { 1 . 5 } { 1 8 }   \\bigg ( \\cfrac { y _ { 2 } ^ { } } { y _ { 1 } ^ { } } \\bigg ) ?$ It is 0.083333... too!"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.085,
            0.914,
            0.123,
            0.934
          ],
          "content": "64"
        }
      ]
    }
